e administration  hasn't even begun yet and there are  people talking about this so I'd like to  say that we're more sophisticated more  determined more set up to stop things  like that but I've seen in the past what  happens when people get scared  you know when we get scared uh we make  very irrational decisions we make lots  of mistakes and I see people being  scared now and um that's worrisome but  not not that I'm someone to point to  things that the late Antonin Scalia ever  said as sort of guiding principles or  anything like that but I found it  interesting in preparing for a  conversation that Antonin Scalia the  conservative Supreme Court justice uh  who seat remains open on the Supreme  Court by the way said a couple of years  ago that quote you were kidding yourself  if you think the same thing referring to  incarceration camps will not happen  again even even he believing that it is  possible that circumstances in America  could lead to that again and I I think  maybe not that he's someone who's Tikku  whose morality and principles I follow  but if even Antonin Scalia recognize  that this could happen again I think  that that means a lot I I agree with you  he made the point a few years ago to law  students in Hawaii and and I agree the  fact that he is of a then that he would  then he would recognize this i think  it's it's very it's very worth noting  and i think that this book to me was a  surprise and then i saw how fast  everything happened and and it's still a  surprise to me how we tend not to look  back at these years and recognize them  there's i just spent the weekend in  arizona there was to incarceration camps  in arizona and to find these camps to  find the remains of these camps I  thought you can find anything on the  internet and one of the camps it was  absolutely impossible to find I had to  be guided by Native Americans that they  believe it or not the camps were on  Indian reservations and Native Americans  had to show me where these camps were so  there's a lot to learn from this  incident and from the book here what I  think you see here there's been many  great books written on the incarceration  novels and nonfiction but this book  shows photographs it really takes us  back to the time you can see what people  look like the day they were picked up  and you can see the conditions in the  camps when they were taken and I think  that helps  understand it helped me understand the  situation much better yeah the  photographs absolutely did disturbing  and illuminating in so many ways we've  been talking about the book unamerican  the incarceration of Japanese Americans  during World War two the book's author  is Richard Cahan it's been so great  speaking to you about this thank you so  much David I appreciate
 

